{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3058","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-bounded-plan-task-artifact-reads.md@sha256:66f8f097e52e61da19a11406b5208a3d517fbe5f5906689c1bf1334699a35a19"},"owner_reference":"3058","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nBounded responses for plan reads, task reads, and artifact content reads.\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nFour read surfaces return whole stored documents with no size guard. A harness commonly caps a single tool response near 10,000 tokens. When a stored document exceeds that cap the call fails outright rather than degrading, and the agent gets nothing \u2014 not a smaller view, not an error it can act on.\n\nThe stored documents are not small by exception. An artifact can be a full architecture specification or a codebase analysis. A ready-task listing returns every task body at once, so its size grows with the number of ready tasks rather than with any one document.\n\nThe four surfaces, confirmed present on `7e6381259`:\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py:95` \u2014 `sam_plan`\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py:167` \u2014 `sam_task`\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:3522` \u2014 `artifact_read`\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:3606` \u2014 `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks`\n\n`sam_schema/server.py` contains no token-budget constant at all. `backlog_view` and `backlog_list` already apply their own budgets; these four do not.\n\nExisting plan and task documentation is silent on content size. It does not acknowledge that large plans or tasks exist, so an agent has no way to anticipate the failure or to ask for less.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. A plan read, a task read, an artifact content read, and a ready-task listing each return a usable response regardless of how large the stored document is.\n2. When a response would exceed the budget, the caller receives a smaller form that tells it what exists and how to ask for a part of it, rather than a failed call.\n3. A caller can reach the whole of a large document across successive calls, with nothing dropped or silently shortened.\n4. A listing reports what is ready without embedding each item's full body.\n5. An agent reading the plan and task documentation learns that large documents exist and how to read one.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. The default item-read path. That is #3057.\n2. Pagination of the ordinal map returned by `backlog_view(map=True)`. That is #3059, and the owner has already scoped its first slice.\n3. Changing what a plan, task, or artifact stores. This is about delivery, not content.\n4. Raising or removing the harness cap. The cap is a given.\n5. Any change to `backlog_view` or `backlog_list`, which already apply budgets.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. No response from the four named surfaces exceeds the configured token budget, whatever the stored document's size.\n2. A response that would exceed the budget is replaced automatically with a smaller form. The caller does not opt in and does not need to know the document's size beforehand.\n3. The smaller form names the exact call that retrieves a specific part.\n4. Every addressable part of an over-budget document is reachable through some sequence of calls. No part is dropped, elided, or truncated away.\n5. A ready-task listing returns each task's identity and the metadata needed to choose one, without that task's full body.\n6. A caller may request a smaller response than the configured default.\n7. The plan and task documentation states that large documents exist and describes how to read one.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. The budget is the engine's single constant, `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` at `plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/models.py:15`. It resolves to `MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS - 500`, falling back to 9,500 when that variable is unset. It must not exceed the harness cap.\n2. Token counts are measured in the `cl100k_base` encoding, matching every other budget in this plugin.\n3. Existing callers of the four surfaces keep working. A response that already fits the budget is unchanged.\n4. Each tool's advertised output schema stays within its per-tool ceiling, and the total across all tools stays within the 13,000-token ceiling asserted by `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py`. Measured headroom on that total is currently 227 tokens, so added response fields are not free.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n1. The item's acceptance criteria say these reads should \"route through the markdown engine\". That names a mechanism. Evaluate whether routing through `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` is the right approach for each of the four surfaces, or whether some need a different treatment \u2014 a plan and a task are structured records, not free markdown, and a ready-task listing is a collection rather than a document.\n2. The criteria cite R3's compact form. Evaluate what the compact form should contain for a plan, for a task, for an artifact, and for a listing. `backlog_view`'s compact manifest is one precedent; it may not transfer.\n3. Address grammar for navigating into a large plan, task, or artifact. `backlog_core` uses dot ordinals. The engine's own renderer emits a different selector grammar. #3059 has already established that mixing the two breaks callers.\n4. Whether the smaller-page request parameter here should match the name and unit the owner chose for #3059, which is `page_budget` in tokens.\n5. Whether `sam_schema` should gain its own budget constant or import the engine's, given R2 requires a single source.\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` \u2014 R1 through R8, the normative behaviour. R2 forbids dropping any addressable node to fit a budget. R3 defines the automatic compact form.\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md` \u2014 which package owns what.\n- `plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/models.py:15` \u2014 the budget constant.\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py` \u2014 the output-schema ceilings.\n- Parent: #3054. Siblings: #3057, #3059, #3078.\n- PR #3551's description lists this issue as outstanding, confirming no prior implementation.\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Who consumes these surfaces? | Agents reading plans, tasks and artifacts through the MCP tools and the DH CLI during the SAM pipeline. | Item description; `sam_schema/server.py` |\n| What observable outcome is expected? | Every call returns something usable, and a large document is reachable in full across calls. | Item acceptance criteria |\n| When does the behaviour trigger? | On every read, when the response would exceed the budget. No caller opt-in. | Contract R3 |\n| Why is this P0? | An unbounded response against a hard cap fails rather than degrades, so the agent gets nothing at all. | Item Context section |\n| Has any of this already been implemented? | No. PR #3551 explicitly lists #3058 as still tracked. | `gh pr view 3551` |\n| Do the four surfaces apply any budget today? | No. `sam_schema/server.py` contains no budget constant. | Verified on `7e6381259` |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- The boundary between this issue and #3078. #3058 covers \"artifact content reads\"; #3078 is titled \"artifact content delivery bypasses the markdown engine entirely\" and states that `artifact_read()` returns raw content with no parsing, navigation or pagination. These appear to describe the same surface. Either #3078 is a subset of this issue, or one of the two should drop artifact reads from its scope. This needs the owner's decision before task decomposition, because two plans touching `artifact_read` would collide the way #3057 and #3059 do.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] Auto mode active. Treated as confirmed per the skill's AUTO_MODE gate; not confirmed by the user in conversation.\n"}